import{_ as a,c as n,o as t,ag as r}from"./chunks/framework.Vyheyqg9.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Node.js: your immediate route","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"learner-guide/node-start.md","filePath":"learner-guide/node-start.md"}'),o={name:"learner-guide/node-start.md"};function s(i,e,d,l,c,u){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="node-js-your-immediate-route" tabindex="-1">Node.js: your immediate route <a class="header-anchor" href="#node-js-your-immediate-route" aria-label="Permalink to “Node.js: your immediate route”">​</a></h1><p><a href="./">Guide home</a> · <a href="./../practice/node-js/core-project.html">Exact project contract</a> · <a href="./../workbench/node-js/">Runtime workbench</a></p><p>You are ready to begin when you can trace a small function that loops over objects, awaits a promise and catches an error. If promise ordering or module imports are uncertain, read that bounded part first and try a tiny trace. No Express, SQL, frontend, cloud or completed CS track is required.</p><p>The following is a recommended just-in-time reading plan. The linked project specification remains the authority for exact caps, statuses, failure cases and evidence. N-01 → N-02 → N-03 → N-04 → N-05 → N-06 is the integrated checkpoint order. Tests begin at N-01; N-05 strengthens them. You can read later explanations before implementing their prerequisite increments.</p><h2 id="n-01-first-cli" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">1 </span>N-01: first CLI <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-01-first-cli" aria-label="Permalink to “1 N-01: first CLI”">​</a></h2><p>Read <a href="./../node-js/module-01-nodejs-foundations.html">Module 1</a>, limited initially to runtime/module formats, package execution and errors; then <a href="./../node-js/module-02-standard-library.html">Module 2</a>, limited to process arguments, paths, file handles and safe output ownership. Scan the <a href="./../node-js/module-04-testing.html">testing chapter</a> for awaiting checks and meaningful assertions.</p><p><strong>Attempt:</strong> one synthetic NDJSON file → pure counts → CLI output. Keep import side effects separate. Read <a href="./../practice/node-js/core-project.html#n-01-—-cli-modules-and-filesystem-ownership">N-01&#39;s exact acceptance</a> before choosing exclusive creation versus replacement. Move on only after valid/empty/malformed/missing-input/output-conflict cases have the expected results, failures leave no partial success and handles close. Keep arguments, JSON, exit codes and protected-fixture observations. Do not treat a working happy path as completion.</p><h2 id="n-02-incremental-bytes" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">2 </span>N-02: incremental bytes <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-02-incremental-bytes" aria-label="Permalink to “2 N-02: incremental bytes”">​</a></h2><p>Read <a href="./../node-js/module-03-events-buffers-streams.html">Module 3</a>: bytes versus characters, UTF-8 decoding, physical framing, iteration, pipeline errors and backpressure. Return to Module 2&#39;s read/close boundaries. Predict split-code-point output before consulting the <a href="./../workbench/node-js/runtime/">runtime collection</a>.</p><p>Implement the existing N-02 limits: 64 KiB chunks, 8 KiB physical records, 10,000 nonblank records and 2 MiB total input. Retain counters rather than full records. Move on after byte-split equivalence, malformed/oversize cases, source/destination failure and slow-consumer demand checks pass. Keep byte fixtures, split schedules, line numbers and bounded-work observations. A tiny fixture does not prove a universal memory ceiling.</p><h2 id="n-03-one-http-lifetime" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">3 </span>N-03: one HTTP lifetime <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-03-one-http-lifetime" aria-label="Permalink to “3 N-03: one HTTP lifetime”">​</a></h2><p>Read <a href="./../node-js/module-05-networking.html">Module 5</a>: raw HTTP request/response streams, status/media/body validation, client body consumption, deadlines and disconnects. Read Module 10&#39;s configuration/startup sections when binding your first listener.</p><p>Reuse the analyzer behind loopback <code>POST /analyze</code>, plus shallow <code>GET /health/live</code>. The HTTP caller cannot choose an arbitrary filesystem path. Move on when valid/400/413/415 outcomes, stalled/disconnected peers, failed bind and delayed outbound body are handled under declared budgets. Retain safe HTTP observations, abort reasons, close/exit results. Browser cookie/CORS behavior is outside a Node-client check.</p><h2 id="n-04-cancellation-and-concurrency" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">4 </span>N-04: cancellation and concurrency <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-04-cancellation-and-concurrency" aria-label="Permalink to “4 N-04: cancellation and concurrency”">​</a></h2><p>Return to <a href="./../node-js/module-01-nodejs-foundations.html">Module 1</a> for bounded admission, result ordering, original errors and cooperative cancellation. Consult the <a href="./../workbench/node-js/runtime/#bounded-concurrency">canonical limiter</a> only after predicting a held-worker schedule; do not fork an incompatible helper.</p><p>Add at most two active local analyses with an item cap. Move on when controllable tasks prove failure stops admission, active peers drain, original <code>undefined</code> rejection survives and pre-abort starts nothing. Keep start/abort/drain sequences and effect counts. Cancellation cannot undo effects or kill an arbitrary promise.</p><h2 id="n-05-meaningful-tests-and-diagnosis" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">5 </span>N-05: meaningful tests and diagnosis <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-05-meaningful-tests-and-diagnosis" aria-label="Permalink to “5 N-05: meaningful tests and diagnosis”">​</a></h2><p>Read <a href="./../node-js/module-04-testing.html">Module 4</a> for pure/integration boundaries, real failures, async assertions and open handles; then <a href="./../node-js/module-11-performance-diagnostics.html">Module 11</a> for event-loop delay/utilization, CPU and memory categories.</p><p>Strengthen prior checks rather than build a second app. Move on when an intentionally broken parser/forgotten await is caught, real temporary-file and HTTP failures are covered, and one controlled stall plus one leaked owner is diagnosed and corrected. Keep the exact workload, failing and passing results, revision/runtime/lock, traces and limits. Commands must terminate naturally.</p><h2 id="n-06-shutdown-and-choose-a-continuation" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">6 </span>N-06: shutdown and choose a continuation <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-06-shutdown-and-choose-a-continuation" aria-label="Permalink to “6 N-06: shutdown and choose a continuation”">​</a></h2><p>Read <a href="./../node-js/module-10-production-ready-applications.html">Module 10</a> for readiness, admission, draining and ordered cleanup; revisit Module 3&#39;s pipeline ownership. Deployment sections can wait.</p><p>Move on when new work is denied during shutdown, active work follows a stated policy, owners close after their last user, repeated shutdown shares its outcome and a stuck owner reaches the external deadline with an honest failure. Keep readiness/admission observations, shutdown order, output/effect state and exit status.</p><h2 id="after-the-analyzer" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">7 </span>After the analyzer <a class="header-anchor" href="#after-the-analyzer" aria-label="Permalink to “7 After the analyzer”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Deepen controlled runtime failures in <a href="./../practice/backend-project.html#p-332">P-332</a>. It follows the analyzer as a learning recommendation even though P-331 appears first in the shared project file.</li><li>Add a service goal: <a href="./../node-js/module-06-express-architecture.html">Express</a> → <a href="./../node-js/module-08-postgresql-mikroorm.html">SQL/PostgreSQL</a> → <a href="./../node-js/module-09-authentication-authorization.html">authorization</a> → <a href="./../practice/backend-project.html#p-331">P-331</a>. Read small SQL examples before implementing transaction races; full database reading is not required for your first query.</li><li>Bring in frontend F01–F04 whenever you want a small public interface; wait for React/HTTP/server authority before F06 authenticated mutations.</li><li>Study CS cost/representation alongside N-02 counters and queue pressure; correctness/invariants alongside N-04 admission/drain. The rest of CS can continue separately.</li><li>Branch into <a href="./tracks.html#python-and-applied-ai">Python and RAG</a> when your goal becomes answering from authorized notes. Workflow tools require explicit authority/receipt design; finishing RAG does not grant write permission.</li><li><a href="./../practice/backend-project.html#p-333">P-333</a> follows a working integrated service and recovery preparation. Redis, MongoDB, GraphQL, brokers, AWS and Kubernetes are requirement-driven continuations, not analyzer prerequisites.</li></ul>',24)])])}const m=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
